---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import docs from '../../generated/docs.json';
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'start-here', text: 'Start here' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'reference', text: 'Reference' },
];
---
<Docs title="Documentation · KeepState" description="KeepState documentation: quickstart, concepts, guides, and the CLI and API references, synced from the product repository." headings={headings}>
  <h1>Documentation</h1>
  <p>
    KeepState runs AI agents inside microVMs and gives them five verbs:
    run, checkpoint, fork, resume, replay. These docs get you from a clean
    machine to a kill-and-resume you performed yourself, then explain the
    two-ledger model everything rests on.
  </p>
  <p class="font-mono text-[11.5px] leading-[1.6] text-taupe m-0 mb-[34px]">
    reference pages are generated from the product repository, never retyped
  </p>
  <h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/quickstart/">Quickstart</a>: clean clone to your first kill-and-resume, about 20 minutes. Synced from the product repo and gate-tested there.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/concepts/">Concepts</a>: sessions, checkpoints, the two ledgers, and why the freeze is atomic.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/claude-code/">Claude Code on KeepState</a>: run a real agent, kill it, watch it resume.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="reference">Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/cli/">CLI reference</a>: all {docs.verbs.length} verbs, usage text straight from the binary.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/api/">API reference</a>: the daemon's {docs.routes.length} HTTP routes.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/limits/">Limits</a>: what KeepState cannot do yet, with the evidence for each limit.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
